# .trivyignore — one check id per line, each with a written justification.
#
# The gate itself stays strict: `severity: HIGH,CRITICAL` and `exit-code: 1`.
# Nothing is disabled to make the scan pass; these two entries record findings
# that were reviewed and judged acceptable for this architecture, with the
# reason. A new finding of either id, in a new place, still fails the run.

# The load balancer is internet-facing by design. The architecture is one
# shared ALB carrying four host rules, fronted by a regional WAF ACL that
# allowlists the Jenkins host and rate-limits /login. Making the ALB internal
# would remove the dev/staging/jenkins hostnames from the public internet, so
# the finding is the intended topology rather than an oversight. Residual risk:
# the ALB is public, and WAF is the only thing between it and the origin.
AWS-0053

# Five port-scoped egress rules on 0.0.0.0/0 (tcp/80 and tcp/443 only) for the
# controller, the build agent, and the Fargate tasks: ECR, SSM, CloudWatch
# Logs, the plugin and package registries, GitHub, and the JNLP callback.
# Trivy flags any 0.0.0.0/0 egress regardless of port, but these are not
# open-to-everything rules. The destinations are dynamic (AWS service
# endpoints and public registries), so pinning CIDRs here would break the
# build agent's package installs rather than harden anything.
#
# Known sharper option, deliberately not taken because it cannot be verified
# without an AWS account: scope the ecs_tasks egress to the VPC CIDR plus the
# VPC interface endpoints, which would close the widest of the five.
AWS-0104
